  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" Reply one is the text \"```json\\n{\\\"x\\\":10}\\n{\\\"y\\\":20}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":30}\". Reply two is the text \"```json\\n{\\\"x\\\":10}\\n{\\\"y\\\":21}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":30}\". For reply one, the rule requires the check to return the object {\"y\":20}, because that is the last object inside the code fence. For reply two, the rule requires the check to return {\"y\":21}, because that is the last object inside the code fence. The check as implemented returns {\"z\":30} for both replies: the fenced pool takes the whole fenced span as one candidate, which is not a valid JSON object, so it skips that pool and falls through to the top-level pool, whose last candidate is {\"z\":30} in both. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. The conjecture would be false if recover_json_object returned {\"y\":20} for reply one and {\"y\":21} for reply two, because then it would be telling the replies apart as the rule requires.",
